                    "updatedAt": "2025-04-29T17:29:12Z",
                    "upvoteCount": 0,
                    "url": "https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/discussions/1766#discussioncomment-12983795"
                },
                {
                    "author": {
                        "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/24854248?u=d09fd974c6720eba3039db6beb2f1c1efecdb51d&v=4",
                        "login": "echasnovski",
                        "resourcePath": "/echasnovski",
                        "url": "https://github.com/echasnovski"
                    },
                    "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
                    "body": "> Thanks but when I tried `:=MiniVisits.write_index(\"/home/krovuxdev/.config/nvim\", {count = 1, latest = os.time() } )`\r\n\r\nPlease read the structure specification more carefully. Especially [this example](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/088d67751584e4168f35bca89bad70ada7ea4636/doc/mini-visits.txt#L188-L201).\r\n\r\n> Also, one more question: I [checked here](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/088d67751584e4168f35bca89bad70ada7ea4636/doc/mini-visits.txt#L701-L715) is there any way to use a number in `iterate_paths(1)`? Something similar to Harpoon, like `harpoon:list():select(1)`, to be able to jump between multiple visits? Is it possible?\r\n\r\nYou can use [custom filter and/or sort](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/088d67751584e4168f35bca89bad70ada7ea4636/doc/mini-visits.txt#L710-L711). Or use [`list_paths()`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/088d67751584e4168f35bca89bad70ada7ea4636/doc/mini-visits.txt#L543) and go from there. Or [use labels](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/088d67751584e4168f35bca89bad70ada7ea4636/doc/mini-visits.txt#L263-L337) for a more Harpoon-like workflow (together with `filter` in `iterate_paths` that filters by the label).",
                    "bodyHTML": "<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Thanks but when I tried <code class=\"notranslate\">:=MiniVisits.write_index(\"/home/krovuxdev/.config/nvim\", {count = 1, latest = os.time() } )</code></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Please read the structure specification more carefully. Especially <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/088d67751584e4168f35bca89bad70ada7ea4636/doc/mini-visits.txt#L188-L201\">this example</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Also, one more question: I <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/088d67751584e4168f35bca89bad70ada7ea4636/doc/mini-visits.txt#L701-L715\">checked here</a> is there any way to use a number in <code class=\"notranslate\">iterate_paths(1)</code>? Something similar to Harpoon, like <code class=\"notranslate\">harpoon:list():select(1)</code>, to be able to jump between multiple visits? Is it possible?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p dir=\"auto\">You can use <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/088d67751584e4168f35bca89bad70ada7ea4636/doc/mini-visits.txt#L710-L711\">custom filter and/or sort</a>. Or use <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/088d67751584e4168f35bca89bad70ada7ea4636/doc/mini-visits.txt#L543\"><code class=\"notranslate\">list_paths()</code></a> and go from there. Or <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/088d67751584e4168f35bca89bad70ada7ea4636/doc/mini-visits.txt#L263-L337\">use labels</a> for a more Harpoon-like workflow (together with <code class=\"notranslate\">filter</code> in <code class=\"notranslate\">iterate_paths</code> that filters by the label).</p>",
                    "bodyText": "Thanks but when I tried :=MiniVisits.write_index(\"/home/krovuxdev/.config/nvim\", {count = 1, latest = os.time() } )\n\nPlease read the structure specification more carefully. Especially this example.\n\nAlso, one more question: I checked here is there any way to use a number in iterate_paths(1)? Something similar to Harpoon, like harpoon:list():select(1), to be able to jump between multiple visits? Is it possible?\n\nYou can use custom filter and/or sort. Or use list_paths() and go from there. Or use labels for a more Harpoon-like workflow (together with filter in iterate_paths that filters by the label).",
